# Routing Engine — Future Design Rules

Apply when implementing `internal/router/`, `internal/rt/`, or `internal/api/`.

## Algorithm (target: sub-20 ms)

- Prefer **RAPTOR or CSA** over preprocessed connection list from `TransitGraph`
- RT delays: **edge weight adjustments** — do not rebuild graph every 15 s
- Benchmark RAPTOR vs CSA on TfNSW (~5 M connections) before committing

## Preprocessing (once at startup)

- Stop → outgoing connections sorted by departure time
- Trip/route lookup tables for RT entity matching
- Rebuild only on static feed reload, not on RT poll

## RT overlay (preferred direction)

- **Lock-free snapshot cache** (`internal/rt.Cache` via `atomic.Pointer`) fed by interval loader
- Loader: `GET` protobuf → `[]rt.Entity` → single `Merge` per tick (one atomic swap)
- **Never** Redis or protobuf on the routing request hot path — read-only `Cache.Get`
- `Get` returns immutable slice refs — callers must not mutate

## HTTP API (planned)

- `net/http` ServeMux or `chi` — no heavy frameworks
- `GET /route?from=&to=&at=` or POST JSON for batch
- Minimal JSON response: legs, transfers, times; defer polylines
- Per-request context timeout aligned with sub-20 ms internal budget

## Transfers (MVP → later)

1. Same-stop transfers only (zero cost, matching stop IDs)
2. `transfers.txt` if in feed
3. OSM walking edges (later)
    - use ai to estimate walking distance
    - car hauling as well

## Hot path guardrails

- No heap allocations per query (use pools for result slices)
- Precomputed time indexes (binary search on departures)
- `testing.B` benchmarks on representative OD pairs

## Static feed reload

- Build new graph in background; swap via `atomic.Pointer[TransitGraph]`
- Expose feed version / parse time on `/health`
